<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese India</h1><p class="st-meta">1505 CE – 1961 CE · capital Goa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Portugal's Estado da India, ruled from Goa for over four centuries and the hub of its maritime empire from East Africa to Macau.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> in 1498, Portugal appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> as first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> in 1505. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate</a> in 1510, making it the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-da-india">Estado da India</a>, an empire of forts and ports including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daman">Daman</a>, Diu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasai">Bassein</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Hormuz</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-da-india">Estado</a> controlled sea trade through the cartaz pass system and fortified posts rather than large territories. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> became a centre of Catholic missions, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> based there and the Goa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> from 1560. Its churches, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-bom-jesus">Basilica of Bom Jesus</a>, are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. A Luso-Indian society emerged, and Konkani and Portuguese were both spoken.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English took most Portuguese posts in the 17th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasai">Bassein</a> in 1739. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daman">Daman</a> and Diu remained Portuguese until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India</a> annexed them by military action in December 1961.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/portuguese-india.jpg" alt="Portuguese India" width="320" height="365"><figcaption>Image: Filpro (original), Sbb1413 (derived work) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IN-GDD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1505 CE – 1961 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Goa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francisco de Almeida, Afonso de Albuquerque</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese, Konkani</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Estado da India, Portuguese State of India, Goa, Daman and Diu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1733&amp;focus=portuguese-india" aria-label="Portuguese India on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6703 1607 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Portuguese India lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7023 1863L4773 1044M7023 1863L7018 1386M7023 1863L7211 2175M7023 1863L7039 2277"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1044h0M7018 1386h0M7211 2175h0M7039 2277h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7023 1863h0"/><path class="map-